From 2d5c8c86f621005e59b2f920d5f635d44a3ded35 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 00:45:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 54/54] inline-3d(#55): follow the foreground tab's demand with
 the panel's 2D/3D element
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The browser never asked the display to go back to 2D. It only ever went quiet:
SubmitBatchWin early-returns on an empty rect list, weaving stops, and the
hardware element stays wherever the last weave left it. Grepping the series for
a display-mode call site returned only the vendored header declarations in
0001. So leaving an inline-3D page left the lens on over flat content, which
reads as soft and ghosted.

Drive it from the inline-3D demand of the ACTIVE TAB OF THE FOREGROUND WINDOW.

Demand is "a registered XRDisplayLayer's target intersects the viewport right
now", not "the document owns a layer" — a layer scrolled off-screen produces no
weave rects and has nothing to weave, so it must read as 2D. That is measured in
XRSession::ReportInline3DRects, one step before the rects lose their identity:
by the time they reach the weave they are flattened into window space by the
surface aggregator with no frame or surface token left, so "which tab" cannot be
recovered downstream. It rides an edge-triggered DisplayXRService::
SetInline3DDemand, never per frame.

The browser aggregates per reporting frame and picks the active tab of the
frontmost window whose contents are VISIBLE — which skips minimized and occluded
windows without a separate test, and lets a background tab's 3D page contribute
nothing. App-level focus is deliberately not required: a visible-but-unfocused
3D page is still on screen and still woven, which is why 0039 exempted inline-3d
from the focus gate in the first place. Decisions are debounced 300 ms, so tab
flicks, page loads, and scrolling an element past the viewport edge cost at most
one hardware transition, and only transitions are sent.

Execution hops browser -> GPU over a new DisplayXRDisplayMode interface. The
decision can only be made in the browser (it is the only process with a tab and
window model) but must be executed on the session that WEAVES, which lives in
the GPU process: the display processor is per-client and the vendor unions its
lens hints across clients, so a request on the browser's own snap/query-only
session would not clear the weaving session's hint.

Ordering is structural rather than incidental. xrRequestDisplayModeDXR is a
HARDWARE-only override and does not stop the weave — asking for 2D too early
swaps ghosting for blur, leaving the woven atlas on screen with the lens off.
Because the demand driving this is the same visible-rect set that produces the
weave rects, by the time it has been false for the whole debounce window
SubmitBatch has been returning early on an empty list for many frames.

The workspace gate is honoured by asking, not by inferring: requestability is a
per-session out-field of xrEnumerateDisplayRenderingModesDXR, resolved once and
cached. Strictly it describes xrRequestDisplayRenderingModeDXR and the
hardware-only call is ungated — we go silent anyway, because under a workspace
the workspace decides and a browser flipping the lens underneath it is the fight
that gate exists to prevent. It is also the only safe reading of a no-op
XR_SUCCESS.

Shutdown hands the panel back flat, best-effort, if a pipe is already bound.
The crash case cannot be covered from here and needs a runtime-side fail-safe:
displayxr-runtime#814. The normal path additionally needs displayxr-runtime#815
— on v2.2.2 a weave present-owner cannot reach hardware 2D at all, because
weave_force_3d_if_needed re-asserts 3D every submit and the pending request is
never drained once submission stops.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
